ime view, search_artifacts over raw sources, recall_past_self, explain_what_you_know_about as a narrative with citations and whats_changed_since as a standup; capture log_signal, the only write and the reason the scope is read-write rather than read-only; and full mirror get_mega_briefing. It earns a guide on a gap rather than a neighbour, because the five memory entries here are agent working memory, Agentic Memory over Redis with FIFO handoff, AIS Memory with a W3C DID identity, MCP Long-Term Memory over Neo4j, Linksee local-first with a forgetting curve and SPM project-scoped with context packs, and none of them ingests a person's ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Grok history across 25 sources with 22 live, Google Calendar in beta and Apple Health plus Spotify announced, nor serves it back with a source and a confidence label on every distilled atom. Pricing is read off the pricing page as a five-tier ladder: Ember free with 50 memories and journal-or-paste ingestion only, Spark 49 dollars a year for 250 to 500 active memories and 10 GB over a one-year window, Founding 149 dollars a year for 2,500 to 10,000 memories and 50 GB over three years with all imports, Counsel 299 dollars a year for up to 35,000 memories and 100 GB over seven years, and Counsel Pro 599 dollars a year with a 300 GB vault and a lifetime window, against a founding cohort the vendor says has 1,456 of 4,000 memberships left. Limits recorded in the guide: a consumer product rather than a business data connector, vault:rw as the only scope so per-scope token issuance is the control that matters, encryption and discretion claims that cannot be checked from outside, self-reported usage figures printed on the vendor's own page at 99.81 percent seven-day uptime, 12,055 calls, 448 connected clients and a 0.9 second p50, and one vault per member with no team or per-client separation. Nothing else was held because nothing else arrived. Core listing check: mcpservers.org still carries /servers/corpusiq-io in